# DEV-004 — executable utility conversion oracles

## Scope

Replace the generic planning text for Base64 and URL encode/decode with exact UTF-8 byte ranges, expected document bytes, failure inputs, and native read-only/rejection procedures. The owner already authorized sequential local work; these definitions and core checks require no desktop, release credentials or product decisions.

Authority: [PR-017 encoding utilities and minimum verification](../../blueprint/PRs/PR-017_BUILT-IN_UTILITIES_COMPARE_FOUNDATION_EXPORT_AND_PRINT.md), [DEV-004](BACKLOG.md), and `utilities_dispatch`/`utilities_pump` in the Windows consumer. Preserve the existing UI and utility transaction boundary.

## Implementation and evidence boundary

Use one checked fixture catalogue for both focused Rust tests and command plans. Verify exact selection replacement, unchanged immutable snapshots, one Undo/Redo, clean/dirty state, cancellation, output limits, invalid decode and stale-revision rejection. Keep native command routing, read-only enforcement and actual saved-file observations pending native execution and independent review. Core tests must never become runtime command acceptance.

No full suite, production build, installer, desktop automation, signing or publication is part of this slice. Retain focused receipts separately from the previous Windows completion bundle.

## Result

Four command plans now share eight exact success vectors and fourteen negative vectors with the real Rust utility core tests. The catalogue binds 12 success/disabled/failure procedures into the generated work definitions, including observed error strings, read-only rejection, whole-document/Unicode selection boundaries, exact output, one Undo/Redo and unchanged saved bytes. The catalogue and source authority hashes are retained in each proposed plan.

Four focused Rust tests and four Python plan/reference-codec checks passed. One initial Rust test-helper compilation error (`unwrap_err` requires a Debug success type) was repaired and its receipt preserved. No product behavior change or native acceptance is claimed. 495 historical commands still lack concrete oracle plans; DEV-004 remains in progress.
